\description{
Robust principal component regression by the RPCR method of Hubert and
Verboven (2003): the robust principal components of the predictors
(\code{\link[=robpca]{robpca()}}), followed by a robust regression of the responses on the
scores. Outlying spectra and wrong reference values have little influence
on the model, and the regression outlier map (\code{\link[=plot_outlier_map]{plot_outlier_map()}})
classifies them.
}
\details{
The method has two steps:
\enumerate{
\item \strong{Robust PCA.} \code{\link[=robpca]{robpca()}} is applied to the predictors with \code{kmax}
components. It gives a robust center \eqn{\hat\mu_x}, the loadings
\eqn{P} and the eigenvalues \eqn{l_a}, and the scores
\eqn{t_i = P^T (x_i - \hat\mu_x)}.
\item \strong{Robust regression.} The responses are regressed on the first
\code{ncomp} scores. With one response, this is the reweighted least
trimmed squares (LTS) regression of \code{\link[robustbase:ltsReg]{robustbase::ltsReg()}}. With
several responses, it is the MCD regression of Rousseeuw, Van Aelst,
Van Driessen and Agullo (2004): least squares from the reweighted MCD
of the scores and responses, then least squares on the observations
whose residual distance is below \eqn{\sqrt{\chi^2_{q, 0.99}}}.
}

The regression coefficients are \eqn{B = P_k A_k}, from the loadings
\eqn{P_k} and the slopes \eqn{A_k} of the regression on the first \code{ncomp}
scores.

\strong{Diagnostics.} As for \code{\link[=rsimpls]{rsimpls()}}: the score distance
\eqn{SD_i = \sqrt{\sum_a t_{ia}^2 / l_a}} has the cut-off
\eqn{\sqrt{\chi^2_{k, 0.975}}}, the residual distance (with one response,
the absolute standardized residual) the cut-off
\eqn{\sqrt{\chi^2_{q, 0.975}}}, and the orthogonal distance
\eqn{OD_i = \|x_i - \hat\mu_x - P t_i\|} the cut-off of \code{\link[=robpca]{robpca()}}.
Observations beyond the score cut-off only are \strong{good leverage} points,
beyond the residual cut-off only \strong{vertical outliers}, and beyond both
\strong{bad leverage} points. The robust \eqn{R^2} of the model (\code{R2}) is
computed on the observations whose orthogonal and residual distances are
both below their cut-offs.

\strong{Number of components.} \code{components} gives, for 1 to \code{kmax}
components, the robust \eqn{R^2} and root mean squared error on the
observations that are regular in every one of these models; use
\code{\link[=robust_rmsecv]{robust_rmsecv()}} for the robust cross-validated error. These models all
come from the same robust PCA, with \code{kmax} components, of which they use
the first \code{ncomp}, so the model also depends on \code{kmax}. With
\code{kmax = ncomp}, the robust PCA has \code{ncomp} components, as in the paper.

\strong{Differences from the paper.}
\itemize{
\item The models with fewer components than \code{kmax} use the first components
of the robust PCA with \code{kmax} components (see above).
\item The residual covariance of the MCD regression is multiplied by the
consistency factor \eqn{0.99 / P(\chi^2_{q+2} \le \chi^2_{q, 0.99})}, as
the observations beyond the cut-off are left out.
}

This is an independent implementation of the published description.
\code{\link[=robpca]{robpca()}}, the LTS and the MCD use random subsets, so use \code{\link[=set.seed]{set.seed()}}
for reproducible results.
}
\examples{
data(forageLIBS)
wl <- suppressWarnings(as.numeric(names(forageLIBS)))
spectra <- forageLIBS[which(wl > 380 & wl < 430)]  # Ca II and Ca I lines
cal <- 1:300
set.seed(1)
fit <- rpcr(spectra[cal, ], forageLIBS$Ca[cal], ncomp = 5)
fit
head(predict(fit, spectra[-cal, ]))
plot_outlier_map(fit)
}
